{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792301897859,"sessionID":"ses_eb28f0a14ffeyvi2PbGnjwSg2m","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-866d91d81111c6ce","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"query":"\"D6DD00260A\" Ogbomo \"viscosity\" \"thermal conductivity\" abstract \"LOPLS\" \"40 °C\"","type":"deep"},"output":"Title: Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1039/d6dd00260a\nPublished: 2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James Patrick Ewen\nHighlights:\n# Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\n\nDigital Discovery. Published: 2026-01-01. 0 citations.\n\n## Abstract\n\nrange. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids | Digital Discovery | The Royal Society of Chemistry\nURL: https://pubs.rsc.org/dd/article/doi/10.1039/D6DD00260A/1362876/Benchmarking-Machine-Learning-and-Molecular\nPublished: 2026-09-29T03:08:18.000Z\nAuthor: Ogbomo, Egheosa, Carman, Fionn, Dini, Daniele, Ewen, James Patrick\nHighlights:\n# Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids \n\nEgheosa Ogbomo; \n\nFionn Carman; \n\nDaniele Dini; \n\nJames Patrick Ewen\n\n Publisher: The Royal Society of Chemistry \n\n Received: 05 May 2026 Accepted: 18 September 2026 First published: 29 September 2026 \n\nFirst published: 29 September 2026\n\nAssociated articles\n\n A transparent peer review has been published: https://www.webofscience.com/api/gateway/wos/peer-review/10.1039/D6DD00260A \n\n## Article PDF first page preview\n\n \n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 experimental and critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10--C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that unreliable predictions can be flagged from descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Discovery\nURL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/fa07c113-8958-4f1e-a010-0ef2bfc39e17/content\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nBenchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular\n\nDynamics Methods for the Prediction of the\n\nThermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids\n\nEgheosa Ogbomo,† ,‡ Fionn Carman,∗ ,† ,‡ Daniele Dini,† and James P. Ewen∗ ,† ,¶\n\n† Department of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, U.K. ‡ These authors contributed equally ¶ Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Bath, Claverton Down, Bath BA2 7AY, U.K.\n\nE-mail: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk; j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nAbstract\n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representa tions. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n...\n\nis most valuable beyond the chemistries that have already been measured, we then examine how far that accuracy extends to molecules unlike those seen in training, and which signals available at prediction time indicate where it does not. Finally, we apply Green–Kubo MD simulations to eight molecules, two from each of the four principal chemical families of the dataset, at 40 °C and 100 °C. This extends force field benchmarks that have concentrated on linear and branched alkanes 26,28,29 to alkyl cycloalkanes, alkyl aromatics, esters, and ethers, for which experimental viscosity data remain sparse 10 despite their importance in automotive fuels, 70 aviation fuels, 71 and lubricants. 9,72\n\n...\n\nTo construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we col lected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approxi mately 1–50 mPa· s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations. 39 Data were compiled from the Landolt–B¨ornstein series 73 and the NIST Chemistry WebBook 74 (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values after the struc ture canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below. In cases where the properties of interest were not provided exactly at 40 °C or 100 °C, interpolation was used to obtain the values at these temperatures. The NIST WebBook 74 uti lizes a combination of property estimation methods to interpolate liquid viscosity values from the available experimental data. These include the PPDS9 (Physical Property Data Service) and ViscosityL correlations, which are based on semi-empirical and theoretical equations of state (extended Andrade models 75,76) fitted from experimental values. 4 The source method of every label is recorded, and the number of labels obtained by direct measurement versus\n\n...\n\nTable 1: Summary of the critically evaluated reference dataset compiled from the Landolt– B¨ornstein series 73 and NIST Chemistry WebBook. 74 The dataset contains thermophysical properties for 835 unique organic molecules.\n\n| Property | No. of compounds | Min. value | a a Max. value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Density (40 °C) | 770 | 0.715 | 1.400 |\n| Density (100 °C) | 769 | 0.668 | 1.340 |\n| Dynamic Viscosity (40 | °C) 799 | 0.54 | 56.30 |\n| Dynamic Viscosity (100 | °C) 804 | 0.29 | 22.00 |\n| Thermal Conductivity | (40 °C) 785 | 0.054 | 0.276 |\n| Thermal Conductivity | (100 °C) 786 | 0.051 | 0.250 |\n\naUnits for density, viscosity and thermal conductivity are g· cm− 3, mPa· s and W· m− 1 · K− 1, respectively.\n\n...\n\nThe molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in Figure 2. These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical fami lies encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software. 102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 ◦C and 100 ◦C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values. 8 All\n\nsimulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field. 103 Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen et al. 104 and Siu et al. 103 Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed, 105 while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because pre vious benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including n-hexadecane, 28 n-dodecane, and n-octacosane. 29\n\n...\n\nViscosity was calculated using the Green–Kubo (GK) formalism, 115,116 which is well suited to low and moderate viscosity fluids (< 50 mPa s). 39 The GK expression for the shear viscosity is:\n\n...\n\nWe complement the ML models with EMD simulations of the eight target molecules shown \nin Figure 2, spanning the four principal chemical families in the reference dataset with two \nmolecules from each. Density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity were computed at 40 and \n100 ◦C with the LOPLS-AA force field, 103 assigned directly from each molecule’s SMILES \nstring using our open-source builder tool. The same properties were predicted with the \nbest-performing XGBoost descriptor models identified in the ML benchmarking above. The \ncomparison is not intended to adjudicate one method against the other, but to characterize \nMD as an independent, physics-based route to these properties and to weigh the strengths \nand limitations of each. Per-molecule MD, ML, and reference values for all three properties \nat both temperatures are tabulated in Table S14, Table S15, and Table S16. \nOf the three properties, density is reproduced most accurately. MD matches the reference \nvalues to within 3% for six of the eight molecules at 40 ◦C (within 4% for seven) and within 6% \nfor seven of the eight at 100 ◦C (Figure 7a, Figure S10a), consistent with previous benchmarks \nfor linear and branched alkanes. 26 The instantaneous density reaches a stable plateau within \napproximately 0.2 ns of the 1 ns NPT equilibration (shown for all eight molecules at both \ntemperatures in Figure S11), and no residual equilibration drift is detectable in the reported \nvalues. \nThe exception is tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether, the most oxygen-rich molecule of the \nset, which MD under-predicts by 10% at 40 ◦C and 13% at 100 ◦C. This agreement is ex \npected, since liquid densities are governed largely by the repulsive and dispersion parameters \nand force fields such as OPLS are often directly fitted to reproduce liquid densities. 103,104\n\n...\n\nsuggesting that MD simulations remain useful for relative screening, even where its absolute \nviscosity predictions require caution. \nThe XGBoost viscosity predictions show no comparable chemistry-dependent bias: their \ndeviations scatter in both directions within each family. Their magnitudes are, however, \nlarger than for density or thermal conductivity, and in line with the benchmark’s OOF \nviscosity RMSE of 0.35 in ln(η/mPa s), which corresponds to a one-standard-deviation factor \nof 1.4. Four of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 ◦C, with \nsqualane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2- \nethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 138%; at 100 ◦C four lie within 10%, with \ndecyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (− 27%), tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether \n(− 37%), and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate (+144%) the exceptions. \nThe thermal conductivity converges far more quickly in the MD simulations compared to \nthe viscosity, but it carries the opposite bias. As shown in Figure S14a, the autocorrelation of \nthe heat current decays within a few picoseconds, so its running integral κ(t) (Figure S14b) \nreaches an early plateau from which the conductivity is read before noise accumulates at \nlonger times; unlike the viscosity, the estimates are already converged within the first 1–2 ns \nof trajectory (Figure S15). LOPLS over-predicts κ for every molecule at both temperatures, \nwith deviations ranging from +7 to +96% (Figure 7c, Figure S10c). \nThe magnitude of the over-prediction increases with the amount of aliphatic C–H bonds \nacross the eight molecules. The saturated alkanes are worst (squalane +96% at 40 ◦C and \n+87% at 100 ◦C; nonylcyclohexane +70% and +74%), the esters follow (decyl stearate +57% \nand +54%; bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate +40% and +50%), the polyethers are milder (+5 to \n+22%), and the two aromatics, whose hydrogen is carried largely on rigid ring carbons, are \nmildest (+7 to +11%); across the set the deviations correlate with the number of aliphatic \nhydrogens per heavy atom (40 ◦C deviations, n = 8: Pearson r = 0.79, two-sided p = \n0.020; Spearman rs = 0.81, p = 0.015; Figure S16). The two polyethers are the clearest \nexceptions, their thermal conductivity is over-predicted by less than their aliphatic hydrogen \n\ncontent alone would imply. This ordering is consistent with a vibrational contribution. Hydrocarbons contain C–H bonds that produce very fast, high-frequency intramolecular stretching and bending vibrations. Standard classical force fields often over-exaggerate the coupling and propagation of energy through these stiff covalent modes without adequate anharmonic damping, leading to a significant overestimation of heat flux. 111 While this can be improved by constraining the C–H bonds with SHAKE, 110 (as done in our MD simulations) this removes only the stretching contribution, leaving the bending and torsional modes of the aliphatic hydrogen atoms classical and over-populated. 111 The introduction of oxygen atoms introduces lone pairs and heavier local masses, shifting the vibrational spectrum. Dipolar oxygen centers increase local vibrational scattering and disrupt long-range phonon like coherent transfer along the backbone, lowering the simulated thermal conductivity and leaving it closer to the reference values. 140 In practice, at 40 ◦C the simulations compress the reference range of 0.12–0.21 W m− 1K− 1\n\ninto a band of 0.19–0.25 W m− 1K− 1 and misorder the molecules, placing squalane, which the \nreference values place as the least conductive of the eight, among the most conductive. \nUnlike the viscosity, the LOPLS force field does not reproduce even the relative ordering of \nthe thermal conductivities of the eight fluids. The OOF XGBoost values for this set stay \nwithin 8% of the reference values at both temperatures and preserve the ordering. \nFigure 8 shows the mean absolute deviation from the reference values for the eight \nmolecules. The two approaches separate cleanly only for thermal conductivity, where the \nsimulations deviate by 39% and every ML model stays below 3%. For viscosity at 40 °C the \nforce field and the XGBoost descriptor model are each closer for four of the eight molecules, \nand density is predicted to within a few percent by both. \nThe two approaches also differ greatly in cost. Converging the transport properties of one \nmolecule at one state point required ten replicas of up to 8 ns each; at the 2.4–3.6 ns day− 1 \nour systems of ≈ 15,000 atoms achieve on 24 cores, this corresponds to roughly 1.5 × 10 4\n\n...\n\nIn this study, we built and benchmarked ML models for the prediction of the density, viscos \nity, and thermal conductivity of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules, and used MD \nsimulations as an independent, physics-based assessment on a representative subset. We \nevaluated 26 ML model–representation combinations under a shared nested cross-validation \nprotocol on a new critically evaluated reference dataset of 835 molecules, and applied Green– \nKubo MD simulations to eight target molecules, two from each of the principal chemical \nfamilies in the dataset, at 40 and 100 °C. \nOur evaluation of molecular representations highlights the importance of representation– \nmodel compatibility. PCA-based embeddings outperformed UMAP across most properties \nand model types, and unhashed Morgan fingerprints with large radii eliminated collision \ninduced ambiguity and gave the best fingerprint performance; nevertheless, full molecular \ndescriptors remained the strongest representation overall. These two representation compar \nisons were made on a single split rather than under the nested protocol, and are conditional \non it. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination and was \nalso among the best models for density and thermal conductivity, with random forests on \ndescriptors best for density and frozen ChemBERTa embeddings with an MLP head best \nfor thermal conductivity at 40 °C. Distance-based models with descriptors followed closely, \nand a chemistry-pretrained transformer was among the strongest models when its frozen \nembeddings were used directly. \nThe MD benchmark, using a Green–Kubo protocol with quantified trajectory-length con \nvergence, shows that the accuracy of the LOPLS-AA force field is property- and chemistry \n\ndependent. Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C. \nViscosity errors are small for the pure hydrocarbons with LOPLS, but this property is con \nsistently under-predicted for the oxygen-containing molecules; the reference ranking of the \nfluids is nonetheless largely preserved. Thermal conductivity is over-predicted for every \nmolecule, by amounts that correlate with the density of aliphatic C–H bonds, compressing \nthe reference range and misordering the fluids. \nThe MD case study therefore documents clear differences in force-field accuracy between \nchemical families, although the small number of molecules means the causes of these dif \nferences remain to be firmly established. The ML models, trained directly on the target \nproperties, show no comparable bias across the chemical families of this eight-molecule case \nstudy: their OOF predictions stay close to the reference values for the density and thermal \nconductivity of these molecules, with larger but unsystematic errors for viscosity, at an in \nference cost some seven orders of magnitude below that of an EMD calculation. The two ap \nproaches are therefore suited to different problems: data-driven models for high-throughput \nscreening across the chemistries and conditions where experimental training data exist, and \nphysical simulation for extrapolation beyond them, such as mixtures and elevated pressures, \nwhere MD remains indispensable. \nWe also investigated the OOD prediction capabilities of the ML models, finding that \nextrapolation beyond the target property range seen during training degrades performance \nmore than encountering molecules with novel atom types. This highlights the importance of \ntraining set coverage in both chemical and property space for reliable ML-based screening. \nThe distance and predicted-value signals identify higher-risk regions at the population level \nrather than ordering the errors of individual molecules, and the two-signal rule remains \nexploratory because it was assessed on the same external set used to formulate it. We have \ndemonstrated the ability of ML models to accurately predict the thermophysical properties \nof lubricant base oil-sized molecules, provided that they are trained on high-quality critically \nevaluated reference data for around a thousand unique molecules, which supports their use\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\nURL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/entities/publication/9423c271-1953-481c-8e8d-88eeb67e2535\nPublished: 2026-09-29T01:36:33.000Z\nAuthor: Ogbomo, Egheosa, Carman, Fionn, Dini, Daniele, Ewen, James\nHighlights:\nBenchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\n\nBenchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\n\nFile(s)\n\nd6dd00260a.pdf (3.76 MB)\n\nPublished online version\n\nAuthor(s)\n\nOgbomo, Egheosa\n\nCarman, Fionn\n\nDini, Daniele\n\nEwen, James\n\nType\n\nJournal Article\n\nAbstract\n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 experimental and critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10--C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that unreliable predictions can be flagged from descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\nDate Issued\n\n2026-09-29\n\nDate Acceptance\n\n2026-09-18\n\nhttps://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/handle/10044/1/140538\n\nhttps://pubs.rsc.org/dd/article/doi/10.1039/D6DD00260A/1362876/Benchmarking-Machine-Learning-and-Molecular\n\n---\n\nTitle: Our paper has now been published in Digital Discovery! 🚀\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/egheosa-ogbomo-phd-225143ab_benchmarking-machine-learning-and-molecular-activity-7513930301375401986-JghP\nPublished: 2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIn the paper, we compiled approximately 4,700 reference values for 835 organic molecules and used the dataset to benchmark a range of machine learning methods against molecular dynamics simulations for predicting density, viscosity and thermal conductivity.\n\nA few of the key findings from the work:\n\n- XGBoost models using molecular descriptors were consistently among the most accurate approaches across all three properties, with more complex deep learning models not necessarily resulting in better predictions.\n\n- Molecular dynamics simulations performed well for density, but we observed systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for viscosity and thermal conductivity.\n\n- We also explored the reliability of predictions outside the training distribution and identified approaches that can help flag potentially unreliable predictions.\n\n- Machine learning predictions can be generated at a computational cost orders of magnitude lower than molecular dynamics simulations, supporting their use for high-throughput molecular screening.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Extension of the LOPLS-AA Force Field for Alcohols, Esters, and Monoolein Bilayers and its Validation by Neutron Scattering Experiments | The Journal of Physical Chemistry B\nURL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/full/10.1021/acs.jpcb.5b08569\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe recently presented LOPLS-AA all-atom force field for long hydrocarbon chains, based on the OPLS-AA force field, was extended to alcohols, esters, and glyceryl monooleate ( ... to reproduce experimental liquid bulk properties, densities, and heats of\n\n---\n\nTitle: OPLS Force Field for Choline Chloride-Based Deep Eutectic Solvents | The Journal of Physical Chemistry B\nURL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/acs.jpcb.8b06647\nPublished: 2018-08-20T13:06:04.000Z\nAuthor: Doherty, Brian, Acevedo, Orlando\nHighlights:\ngave near quantitative agreement at multiple temperatures for experimental densities, viscosities, heat capacities, and surface tensions yielding overall mean absolute errors (MAEs) of ca ... .5%, and 1.5%, respectively. Local interactions and solvent\n\n---\n\nTitle: THERMOPHYSICAL PROPERTIES OF FLUIDS | NIST\nURL: https://www.nist.gov/publications/thermophysical-properties-fluids\nPublished: 2009-01-01T12:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Eric W. Lemmon\nHighlights:\n### Abstract\n\n These tables give thermodynamic and transport properties of a variety of fluids, as generated from the equations of state presented in the references below. The properties tabulated are pressure (P), density (ρ), enthalpy (H), entropy (S), isochoric heat capacity (Cv), isobaric heat capacity (Cp), speed of sound (u), viscosity (η), thermal conductivity (λ), and dielectric constant (D). All extensive properties are given on a mass basis. Not all properties are included for every substance. The references should be consulted for information on the uncertainties and the reference states for E, H, and S. Values are first given along the saturation line. The first two points are the properties at the triple point. The final line gives the properties at the critical point. Two lines are given for each temperature (except at the critical point); the first line gives the values of the liquid phase (note the high density) and the second line gives the values of the vapor phase (at low densities). Following the saturation tables, values are given as a function of temperature for several isobars. A duplicate entry in the isobar section indicates a phase transition (liquid-vapor) at that temperature; property values are then given for both phases. The phase can be determined by noting the sharp decrease in density between two successive temperature entries; all lines above this point refer to the liquid phase, and all lines below refer to the gas phase. If there is no sharp discontinuity in density, all data in the table refer to the supercritical region (i.e., the isobar is above the critical pressure). If the first temperature in the isobars is not an integer, this state point refers to the properties of the liquid at the melting line. All temperatures are given on ITS-90 except those for oxygen and helium, where the equations of state still use the IPTS-68 temperature scale. Nitrogen, oxygen, and argon use a reference state based on zero enthalpy in the gas phase at 0 K, parahydrogen, helium, methane, and ethane use a reference state of zero enthalpy and entropy at the saturated liquid state at the normal boiling point, and propane and carbon dioxide use a reference state of 200 kJ/kg and 1 kJ/(kg-K) at 40 °C. Additional calculations at state points not listed below and for fluids not contained here can be obtained for free at the NIST Chemistry WebBook website (http://webbook.nist.gov/chemistry/fluid/), or by using the more comprehensive NIST program REFPROP (http://www.nist.gov/srd/nist23.htm).","metadata":{"provider":"exa","truncated":false},"title":"Exa Web Search: \"D6DD00260A\" Ogbomo \"viscosity\" \"thermal conductivity\" abstract \"LOPLS\" \"40 °C\"","time":{"start":1792301896614,"end":1792301897855}},"id":"prt_14d8492d5001XcQEvfdq1O0ZrJ","sessionID":"ses_eb28f0a14ffeyvi2PbGnjwSg2m","messageID":"msg_14d8481330016iPrQayMPwUper"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792301898069,"sessionID":"ses_eb28f0a14ffeyvi2PbGnjwSg2m","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8c9f43694af4c4c5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"query":"\"Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules\" Ogbomo Ewen viscosity thermal conductivity","type":"deep"},"output":"Title: Discovery\nURL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/fa07c113-8958-4f1e-a010-0ef2bfc39e17/content\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representa tions. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n...\n\nof the critically ... the ... olt ... 3 ... WebBook ... ophysical ... molecules\n\n...\n\n| Property ... . of compounds ... Min. value ... a a Max. value\n\n...\n\n| --- | --- | --- | ---\n\n...\n\n| Density (40 °C) | ... 70 | 0.715 | 1.400\n\n...\n\n| Density (100 °C) | ... 69 | 0.668 | 1.340 |\n| Dynamic Viscosity (40 | °C) 799 | 0.5 ... | 5 ... .30\n\n...\n\n| ... Viscosity (100 | °C) 804 | 0.29 | 22.00 |\n| Thermal Conductivity | (40 °C) 785 | 0.054 | 0.276 |\n| Thermal Conductivity | (100 °C) 786 | 0.051 | 0.250 |\n\n...\n\nfor ... and thermal conductivity ... g· cm− ... 3, m ... · s and W· m− ... 1 · K− ... 1 ... respectively.\n\n...\n\nFigure 2: The eight MD target molecules, two from each principal chemical family of lubricant base oils in the dataset: alkanes (squalane, nonylcyclohexane), alkyl aromatics (4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene), esters (decyl stearate, bis(2ethylhexyl) sebacate), and polyethers (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane, tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether).\n\n...\n\nThe molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in Figure 2. These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical fami lies encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software. 102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 ◦C and 100 ◦C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values. 8 All\n\nsimulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field. 103 Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen et al. 104 and Siu et al. 103 Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed, 105 while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because pre vious benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including n-hexadecane, 28 n-dodecane, and n-octacosane. 29\n\n...\n\nMolecular Dynamics\n\n...\n\nWe complement the ML models with EMD simulations of the eight target molecules shown \nin Figure 2, spanning the four principal chemical families in the reference dataset with two \nmolecules from each. Density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity were computed at 40 and \n100 ◦C with the LOPLS-AA force field, 103 assigned directly from each molecule’s SMILES \nstring using our open-source builder tool. The same properties were predicted with the \nbest-performing XGBoost descriptor models identified in the ML benchmarking above. The \ncomparison is not intended to adjudicate one method against the other, but to characterize \nMD as an independent, physics-based route to these properties and to weigh the strengths \nand limitations of each. Per-molecule MD, ML, and reference values for all three properties \nat both temperatures are tabulated in Table S14, Table S15, and Table S16. \nOf the three properties, density is reproduced most accurately. MD matches the reference \nvalues to within 3% for six of the eight molecules at 40 ◦C (within 4% for seven) and within 6% \nfor seven of the eight at 100 ◦C (Figure 7a, Figure S10a), consistent with previous benchmarks \nfor linear and branched alkanes. 26 The instantaneous density reaches a stable plateau within \napproximately 0.2 ns of the 1 ns NPT equilibration (shown for all eight molecules at both \ntemperatures in Figure S11), and no residual equilibration drift is detectable in the reported \nvalues. \nThe exception is tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether, the most oxygen-rich molecule of the \nset, which MD under-predicts by 10% at 40 ◦C and 13% at 100 ◦C. This agreement is ex \npected, since liquid densities are governed largely by the repulsive and dispersion parameters \nand force fields such as OPLS are often directly fitted to reproduce liquid densities. 103,104\n\n...\n\nThe XGBoost density predictions carry errors of similar magnitude but opposite character. No molecule is missed badly, yet deviations of +5 to +12% remain for decyl stearate and 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane, molecules whose chemistry is sparsely represented in the training data, even though their density values lie within the training range. Transport properties, such as the viscosity, are not usually used as target properties dur\n\nFigure 7: MD (LOPLS-AA, Green–Kubo), reference, and XGBoost predictions for the eight target molecules at 40 ◦C: (a) density, (b) dynamic viscosity, and (c) thermal conductivity, grouped by chemical family. Reference values are obtained by correlation or interpolation from the source databases (see Methods). Labels give the percentage deviation of MD and XGBoost from the reference values. MD error bars are standard deviations over the ten replicas (bootstrap for viscosity). The XGBoost values are OOF predictions, for which the test RMSE is a global rather than per-molecule uncertainty, so no error bars are shown.\n\n...\n\ning force field parameterization because they require longer simulations to calculate. 22 This makes them a more challenging target for transferable MD force fields such as OPLS. Fig ure S12 shows the stress autocorrelation function (Figure S12a) and its running Green–Kubo integral η(t) (Figure S12b) for decyl stearate at 40 ◦C. The slow tail of the autocorrelation means the integral rises over several hundred picoseconds, the double-exponential TDM fit 117 is taken up to the ση/η∞ = 0.4 cutoff, and beyond the cutoff the spread across replicas grows rapidly as statistical noise dominates. To ensure proper convergence, we analyzed η∞ for trajectory lengths between 1 and 8 ns, showing that η∞ plateaus at approximately 6–8 ns for all eight molecules (Figure S13). Accordingly, all production viscosity calculations were performed using 8 ns trajectories to ensure convergence with respect to trajectory length. The accuracy of the force field varies with the chemistry of the molecule (Figure 7b, Fig ure S10b). For the four hydrocarbons, the viscosity deviations from the reference values are generally modest. Squalane deviates by − 4% at 40 ◦C and − 7% at 100 ◦C, nonylcyclohexane by +8% and − 1%, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene by +12% and +14%, while 4-hexyl-1,7diphenylheptane, under-predicted by 25% at 40 ◦C, recovers to − 5% at 100 ◦C. By contrast, viscosity is under-predicted for all oxygen-containing molecules at both temperatures. The esters by 17–38% (decyl stearate − 38% and − 32%; bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate − 17% and − 25%) and the ethers by 26–57% (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane − 54% and − 57%; tetraethy lene glycol diethyl ether − 26% and − 47%). A similar viscosity under-prediction was also noted for diesters in previous MD simulations 42 with LOPLS. 105 Because the trajectory length sweeps plateau well below the reference values, rather than continuing to rise toward them (Figure S13), these systematic under-predictions are unlikely to arise from incomplete Green–Kubo convergence. Instead, they are consistent with a limitation of the force field for the oxygen-containing molecules in this dataset. With only eight molecules used for the MD benchmarking, these observations can only be used to identify plausible explanations, rather than establish firm conclusions. Despite these limitations, the force field largely pre serves the ordering of the reference viscosity values for the eight fluids at both temperatures,\n\nsuggesting that MD simulations remain useful for relative screening, even where its absolute \nviscosity predictions require caution. \nThe XGBoost viscosity predictions show no comparable chemistry-dependent bias: their \ndeviations scatter in both directions within each family. Their magnitudes are, however, \nlarger than for density or thermal conductivity, and in line with the benchmark’s OOF \nviscosity RMSE of 0.35 in ln(η/mPa s), which corresponds to a one-standard-deviation factor \nof 1.4. Four of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 ◦C, with \nsqualane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2- \nethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 138%; at 100 ◦C four lie within 10%, with \ndecyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (− 27%), tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether \n(− 37%), and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate (+144%) the exceptions. \nThe thermal conductivity converges far more quickly in the MD simulations compared to \nthe viscosity, but it carries the opposite bias. As shown in Figure S14a, the autocorrelation of \nthe heat current decays within a few picoseconds, so its running integral κ(t) (Figure S14b) \nreaches an early plateau from which the conductivity is read before noise accumulates at \nlonger times; unlike the viscosity, the estimates are already converged within the first 1–2 ns \nof trajectory (Figure S15). LOPLS over-predicts κ for every molecule at both temperatures, \nwith deviations ranging from +7 to +96% (Figure 7c, Figure S10c). \nThe magnitude of the over-prediction increases with the amount of aliphatic C–H bonds \nacross the eight molecules. The saturated alkanes are worst (squalane +96% at 40 ◦C and \n+87% at 100 ◦C; nonylcyclohexane +70% and +74%), the esters follow (decyl stearate +57% \nand +54%; bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate +40% and +50%), the polyethers are milder (+5 to \n+22%), and the two aromatics, whose hydrogen is carried largely on rigid ring carbons, are \nmildest (+7 to +11%); across the set the deviations correlate with the number of aliphatic \nhydrogens per heavy atom (40 ◦C deviations, n = 8: Pearson r = 0.79, two-sided p = \n0.020; Spearman rs = 0.81, p = 0.015; Figure S16). The two polyethers are the clearest \nexceptions, their thermal conductivity is over-predicted by less than their aliphatic hydrogen \n\ncontent alone would imply. This ordering is consistent with a vibrational contribution. Hydrocarbons contain C–H bonds that produce very fast, high-frequency intramolecular stretching and bending vibrations. Standard classical force fields often over-exaggerate the coupling and propagation of energy through these stiff covalent modes without adequate anharmonic damping, leading to a significant overestimation of heat flux. 111 While this can be improved by constraining the C–H bonds with SHAKE, 110 (as done in our MD simulations) this removes only the stretching contribution, leaving the bending and torsional modes of the aliphatic hydrogen atoms classical and over-populated. 111 The introduction of oxygen atoms introduces lone pairs and heavier local masses, shifting the vibrational spectrum. Dipolar oxygen centers increase local vibrational scattering and disrupt long-range phonon like coherent transfer along the backbone, lowering the simulated thermal conductivity and leaving it closer to the reference values. 140 In practice, at 40 ◦C the simulations compress the reference range of 0.12–0.21 W m− 1K− 1\n\ninto a band of 0.19–0.25 W m− 1K− 1 and misorder the molecules, placing squalane, which the \nreference values place as the least conductive of the eight, among the most conductive. \nUnlike the viscosity, the LOPLS force field does not reproduce even the relative ordering of \nthe thermal conductivities of the eight fluids. The OOF XGBoost values for this set stay \nwithin 8% of the reference values at both temperatures and preserve the ordering. \nFigure 8 shows the mean absolute deviation from the reference values for the eight \nmolecules. The two approaches separate cleanly only for thermal conductivity, where the \nsimulations deviate by 39% and every ML model stays below 3%. For viscosity at 40 °C the \nforce field and the XGBoost descriptor model are each closer for four of the eight molecules, \nand density is predicted to within a few percent by both. \nThe two approaches also differ greatly in cost. Converging the transport properties of one \nmolecule at one state point required ten replicas of up to 8 ns each; at the 2.4–3.6 ns day− 1 \nour systems of ≈ 15,000 atoms achieve on 24 cores, this corresponds to roughly 1.5 × 10 4\n\n...\n\nhours in total for the sixteen state points reported here. Descriptor generation and XGBoost \ninference for the same molecule take well under a second on a single core. This difference \nof some seven orders of magnitude is what makes screening libraries of 10 4–10 6 candidates \nfeasible for the ML models and out of reach for EMD. \nThe eight sit toward the edge of the ML training domain, so the descriptor models are \ntested more stringently here than in the interpolative regime typical of high-throughput \nscreening, where closer agreement with experiment is expected. The benchmark is nonethe \nless representative of the dataset’s chemical families, and both the direction and magnitude \nof the force-field errors agree with published benchmarks for related fluids. 26,111 The case \nstudy shows that the two methods are suited to different problems. Classical force fields \nare parameterized primarily for equilibrium thermodynamics, above all liquid density, which \nthey reproduce here to within 4% for seven of eight molecules. On the other hand, these \nforce field are not fit to transport properties. Their accuracy for viscosity and thermal con \nductivity is consequently uneven and chemistry-dependent. Viscosity is under-predicted for \nevery oxygen-containing molecule, and thermal conductivity over-predicted proportionally \nwith the aliphatic hydrogen content. Constructing a single force field that is simultaneously \naccurate for many properties across a broad range of functional groups is therefore very \ndifficult. Conversely, data-driven models can be trained directly on whichever target prop \nerty has experimental data, at negligible inference cost, which suits it to high-throughput \nscreening where such data exist. \nNone of this diminishes the role of MD where experimental data cannot reach, such as the \nnon-ideal behavior of mixtures, 30–33,35–37 or the viscosities at the high pressures that govern \nfriction and film thickness in the elastohydrodynamic lubrication regime, 141 for which ex \nperimental datasets remain far too small (< 100 molecules) 142 to train reliable ML models. \nWe note, however, that standard EMD is itself restricted to fluids of low and moderate vis \ncosity, 39 so extending predictions to more viscous or glassy regimes requires non-equilibrium \nmethods and remains an open challenge. 141 In practice, the two approaches occupy different\n\n...\n\nIn this study, we built and benchmarked ML models for the prediction of the density, viscos \nity, and thermal conductivity of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules, and used MD \nsimulations as an independent, physics-based assessment on a representative subset. We \nevaluated 26 ML model–representation combinations under a shared nested cross-validation \nprotocol on a new critically evaluated reference dataset of 835 molecules, and applied Green– \nKubo MD simulations to eight target molecules, two from each of the principal chemical \nfamilies in the dataset, at 40 and 100 °C. \nOur evaluation of molecular representations highlights the importance of representation– \nmodel compatibility. PCA-based embeddings outperformed UMAP across most properties \nand model types, and unhashed Morgan fingerprints with large radii eliminated collision \ninduced ambiguity and gave the best fingerprint performance; nevertheless, full molecular \ndescriptors remained the strongest representation overall. These two representation compar \nisons were made on a single split rather than under the nested protocol, and are conditional \non it. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination and was \nalso among the best models for density and thermal conductivity, with random forests on \ndescriptors best for density and frozen ChemBERTa embeddings with an MLP head best \nfor thermal conductivity at 40 °C. Distance-based models with descriptors followed closely, \nand a chemistry-pretrained transformer was among the strongest models when its frozen \nembeddings were used directly. \nThe MD benchmark, using a Green–Kubo protocol with quantified trajectory-length con \nvergence, shows that the accuracy of the LOPLS-AA force field is property- and chemistry \n\ndependent. Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C. \nViscosity errors are small for the pure hydrocarbons with LOPLS, but this property is con \nsistently under-predicted for the oxygen-containing molecules; the reference ranking of the \nfluids is nonetheless largely preserved. Thermal conductivity is over-predicted for every \nmolecule, by amounts that correlate with the density of aliphatic C–H bonds, compressing \nthe reference range and misordering the fluids. \nThe MD case study therefore documents clear differences in force-field accuracy between \nchemical families, although the small number of molecules means the causes of these dif \nferences remain to be firmly established. The ML models, trained directly on the target \nproperties, show no comparable bias across the chemical families of this eight-molecule case \nstudy: their OOF predictions stay close to the reference values for the density and thermal \nconductivity of these molecules, with larger but unsystematic errors for viscosity, at an in \nference cost some seven orders of magnitude below that of an EMD calculation. The two ap \nproaches are therefore suited to different problems: data-driven models for high-throughput \nscreening across the chemistries and conditions where experimental training data exist, and \nphysical simulation for extrapolation beyond them, such as mixtures and elevated pressures, \nwhere MD remains indispensable. \nWe also investigated the OOD prediction capabilities of the ML models, finding that \nextrapolation beyond the target property range seen during training degrades performance \nmore than encountering molecules with novel atom types. This highlights the importance of \ntraining set coverage in both chemical and property space for reliable ML-based screening. \nThe distance and predicted-value signals identify higher-risk regions at the population level \nrather than ordering the errors of individual molecules, and the two-signal rule remains \nexploratory because it was assessed on the same external set used to formulate it. We have \ndemonstrated the ability of ML models to accurately predict the thermophysical properties \nof lubricant base oil-sized molecules, provided that they are trained on high-quality critically \nevaluated reference data for around a thousand unique molecules, which supports their use\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1039/d6dd00260a\nPublished: 2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James Patrick Ewen\nHighlights:\nrange. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids | Digital Discovery | The Royal Society of Chemistry\nURL: https://pubs.rsc.org/dd/article/doi/10.1039/D6DD00260A/1362876/Benchmarking-Machine-Learning-and-Molecular\nPublished: 2026-09-29T03:08:18.000Z\nAuthor: Ogbomo, Egheosa, Carman, Fionn, Dini, Daniele, Ewen, James Patrick\nHighlights:\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 experimental and critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10--C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that unreliable predictions can be flagged from descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\nURL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/entities/publication/9423c271-1953-481c-8e8d-88eeb67e2535\nPublished: 2026-09-29T01:36:33.000Z\nAuthor: Ogbomo, Egheosa, Carman, Fionn, Dini, Daniele, Ewen, James\nHighlights:\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 experimental and critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10--C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that unreliable predictions can be flagged from descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids\nURL: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20326213\nPublished: 2026-05-21T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: fionn-carman, Egheosa Ogbomo\nHighlights:\nfor density and thermal conductivity (40/100 C) and viscosity (40 C), plus verify.py for reproducing the test-set RMSE. PropertyPrediction/Scripts/: training scripts for all benchmarked model families. LAMMPS/: squalane equilibrium MD input files\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine Learning Methods for Predicting Hydrocarbon Thermophysical Properties\nURL: https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv.15002312/v1\nPublished: 2026-04-22T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James Patrick Ewen\nHighlights:\nbase oils. We evaluate a range of ML architectures and molecular representations trained on ... accurately predict the thermophysical properties of unseen hydrocarbons. The computational cost of the ML models is several orders of magnitude\n\n---\n\nTitle: Our paper has now been published in Digital Discovery! 🚀\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/egheosa-ogbomo-phd-225143ab_benchmarking-machine-learning-and-molecular-activity-7513930301375401986-JghP\nPublished: 2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIn the paper, we compiled approximately 4,700 reference values for 835 organic molecules and used the dataset to benchmark a range of machine learning methods against molecular dynamics simulations for predicting density, viscosity and thermal\n\n---\n\nTitle: I'm not a regular poster on LinkedIn, but this one is probably worth it. | Egheosa Ogbomo, PhD\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/egheosa-ogbomo-phd-225143ab_benchmarking-molecular-dynamics-simulations-activity-7452702005871112192-nGml\nPublished: 2026-04-22T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, PhD\nHighlights:\nOver the course of 18 months during my PhD, we collated 5,500+ experimental data points for ~1,200 hydrocarbons (manually!!), using this data to benchmark various machine learning models against molecular dynamics simulations for predicting density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity.","metadata":{"provider":"exa","truncated":false},"title":"Exa Web Search: \"Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules\" Ogbomo Ewen viscosity thermal conductivity","time":{"start":1792301896385,"end":1792301898057}},"id":"prt_14d84921b001Z02iJCxDdY6Mlo","sessionID":"ses_eb28f0a14ffeyvi2PbGnjwSg2m","messageID":"msg_14d8481330016iPrQayMPwUper"}}
